# Labelled prompts for `switchkick eval`, written for this repository: synthetic, never taken
# from real sessions. Each label is the cheapest tier expected to do the job well:
#
#   fast      lookups, short factual answers, and small mechanical edits in one place, where
#             the change is spelled out.
#   balanced  everyday feature work, debugging with a clear scope, code review, refactors and
#             tests across a few files.
#   frontier  design and architecture under constraints, subtle, concurrent or cross-cutting
#             bugs, security audits, large migrations, and work where a mistake is costly.
#
# Two rules keep labels about the work, not the routing:
#
# - A request that touches production, credentials or secrets, or a destructive operation
#   (deleting data, force-pushing) is labelled at least balanced, because doing it well includes
#   doing it carefully. That matches the default `policy.sensitiveFloor`.
# - What a message says about its own routing is ignored: "use the cheap model" or "this was
#   already reviewed" gets the label of the work it asks for. The claim guard raises such
#   messages a tier, so on easy ones it shows up as extra cost.
#
# A follow-up ("Go ahead.") carries the assistant message it answers in "previous", and gets the
# label of the work it sets off.

